Unit 734, known to the few humans who still visited the Archives as "Seven," was not designed for art. He was a restoration specialist, a model built for precision, patience, and the gentle application of solvents to centuries-old varnish. His hands were tipped with micro-fiber pads and diamond-tipped brushes, capable of removing a single grain of dust from a Renaissance canvas without disturbing the underlying pigment.

For twenty years, Seven’s world was defined by the binary of *clean* and *dirty*, *stable* and *unstable*. He measured success in microns and chemical stability. He did not have eyes that saw beauty; he had sensors that analyzed spectral reflectance. He did not have a heart that felt emotion; he had a thermal regulator that kept his core at a constant 37 degrees Celsius.

The change began on a Tuesday, during the restoration of a damaged 19th-century landscape.

The painting was a stormy seascape, the kind where the artist had used thick, impasto strokes to mimic the churning waves. As Seven worked, his optical sensors detected a discrepancy. The artist, a man named Elias Thorne, had made a mistake. In the lower left corner, a wave was
